## 2026-05-19 – Capstone architecture audit and gap analysis

**Context:**  
I was working on my Integration component for our AI-Powered Quiz Generator capstone project. Our project uses Airtable, n8n, Flowise, Groq, and GitHub. I needed to evaluate whether our system was actually ready for Checkpoint 2, which requires one complete record flowing through all four team components end-to-end without manual intervention.

I had access to:
- our Airtable schema
- workflow architecture
- current testing results
- known workflow limitations
- component responsibilities

**Prompt:**  

> I need you to act as a capstone project advisor for a university AI integration course. I'm going to describe my group project, and I need you to interview me about its current state, then produce a structured gap analysis.
>
> My project uses: n8n (workflow automation), Flowise (LLM chains), Airtable (database), Groq/HuggingFace (AI APIs), and GitHub.
>
> The team has 4 components: Ingestion, AI Core, Specialist, and Integration.
>
> Checkpoint 2 is next week. The requirement is: one complete record flows through all 4 components end-to-end without manual intervention.
>
> Please interview me with the following questions, ONE AT A TIME. Wait for my answer before asking the next question.
>
> 1. What is your project name and what problem does it solve?
> 2. What is your role/component on the team?
> 3. Describe your Airtable schema.
> 4. What is currently working?
> 5. What is not yet working?
> 6. Have you tested Ingestion → AI Core handoff?
> 7. Have you tested AI Core → Specialist handoff?
> 8. Are there field mismatches?
> 9. What test data exists?
> 10. Biggest Checkpoint 2 concern?
>
> After all 10 answers, produce:
> - Checkpoint 2 Readiness Assessment
> - Working components
> - Critical gaps
> - Schema issues
> - Recommended fix order
> - Test data gaps

**Result:**  
The AI produced a structured readiness audit and marked the project as **AT RISK**.

Key findings:
- Manual n8n execution is the biggest blocker
- Multiple choice generation is unreliable
- Quiz Results overlaps with Performance responsibilities
- Error logging exists in schema but is not implemented
- Some scoring/performance fields are inconsistently populated
- Edge case testing is incomplete

It also recommended concrete fixes and assigned likely ownership by team member.

**Evaluation:**  
This interaction was highly useful because it forced a structured diagnostic review instead of vague troubleshooting.

The AI accurately identified our biggest technical blocker (manual workflow triggering) and several architectural weaknesses.

However, some recommendations assumed architectural cleanup (such as schema consolidation) that may not be necessary before the checkpoint deadline.

Overall, the audit was realistic, accurate, and actionable.

**What I changed:**  
I did not accept all recommendations directly.

I adjusted the strategy by:
- prioritizing practical fixes over architectural redesign
- treating Quiz Results and Errors as support tables rather than immediate restructuring priorities
- explicitly adding project deadline constraints
- clarifying exact schema details
- refining AI context so recommendations matched our real implementation stage

**What I learned:**  
AI performs much better when given exact system architecture, constraints, and schema context.

Instead of asking a broad troubleshooting question, structuring the interaction as a guided audit produced much more useful engineering advice.

I also learned that AI can support architecture review, debugging strategy, and systems planning—not just code generation.

